  "thread": [
   {
    "role": "user",
    "content": "Where does ITER's machine assembly actually stand?",
    "ts": "2026-11-07T16:36:51+00:00"
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "content": "As of 29 July 2026 the ITER Organization had installed the sixth of nine tokamak sector modules in the tokamak pit, putting roughly two-thirds of the torus-shaped core in place. The organisation says the milestone landed almost six months ahead of schedule, and the module involved weighed about 1,100 tonnes and was transferred from the Assembly Hall in a coordinated lifting operation. Sources: 'Two-thirds of ITER tokamak core now in place' (ITER, 29 Jul 2026) and ANS Nuclear Newswire's 9 Sep 2026 assembly report.",
    "ts": "2026-11-07T16:36:51+00:00"
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "content": "Is the US roadmap to fusion deployment by the mid-2030s credible?",
    "ts": "2026-11-07T16:36:51+00:00"
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "content": "Reuters (25 Aug 2026) reports the US government launched a strategy to catalyse early-stage fusion plants by the mid-2030s, and that success hinges on more federal funding. Developers point to unsolved engineering: neutron-resistant structural materials, tritium separation and reprocessing, and components that survive constant high-energy neutron flux. Robb Hughes of Realta Fusion framed it as \"The plan isn't the problem. We all agree on the plan. The X factor is the funding.\" The roadmap leans on existing DOE programmes such as Milestone-Based Fusion Development and INFUSE. Treat the timeline as policy intent, not a committed delivery date.",
    "ts": "2026-11-07T16:36:51+00:00"
   }
  ],
